WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.707
Blazor forms and components often collect
user input and trigger state-changing

2
00:00:05.787 --> 00:00:06.391
actions.

3
00:00:06.471 --> 00:00:11.678
The interaction may feel strongly typed
because values are bound to .NET objects,

4
00:00:11.758 --> 00:00:16.512
but bound form data is still input from
outside the trusted decision point.

5
00:00:16.592 --> 00:00:21.723
Binding makes data convenient to process;
it does not make it safe or authorized.

6
00:00:21.803 --> 00:00:24.746
Client-side validation improves usability.

7
00:00:24.826 --> 00:00:27.467
It can catch missing fields, bad formats,

8
00:00:27.547 --> 00:00:30.415
and common mistakes before the user
submits.

9
00:00:30.495 --> 00:00:33.061
It should not be treated as enforcement.

10
00:00:33.141 --> 00:00:37.517
The server or trusted backend must
validate required fields, types,

11
00:00:37.597 --> 00:00:40.390
formats, lengths, ranges, relationships,

12
00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.639
and business rules before performing the
change.

13
00:00:43.719 --> 00:00:49.001
State-changing operations should verify
authorization and input validity at the

14
00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:51.043
point where the change is made.

15
00:00:51.123 --> 00:00:53.463
A component may prepare the request,

16
00:00:53.543 --> 00:00:58.900
but the backend should confirm that the
current identity may perform the operation,

17
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.622
that the target resource is within scope,

18
00:01:01.702 --> 00:01:05.475
and that the submitted data matches the
expected contract.

19
00:01:05.555 --> 00:01:11.214
Cookie-authenticated browser workflows may
need CSRF protection for unsafe requests.

20
00:01:11.294 --> 00:01:16.577
The details depend on how the Blazor
application is hosted and how backend calls are

21
00:01:16.657 --> 00:01:21.788
made, but the design should not assume
that authentication alone proves request

22
00:01:21.868 --> 00:01:24.887
intent for every browser-driven state
change.

23
00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:29.495
File uploads and large request bodies need
explicit limits and review.

24
00:01:29.575 --> 00:01:33.499
Teams should define accepted business
purpose, size limits,

25
00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:35.993
content handling, storage location,

26
00:01:36.073 --> 00:01:40.601
scanning or review workflow where
appropriate, and failure behavior.

27
00:01:40.681 --> 00:01:45.435
Upload handling should be designed as a
security and reliability feature,

28
00:01:45.515 --> 00:01:46.874
not just a form input.

29
00:01:46.954 --> 00:01:50.802
The baseline is consistent: collect input
in the component,

30
00:01:50.882 --> 00:01:54.052
validate for usability in the client when
useful,

31
00:01:54.132 --> 00:01:59.414
then enforce authorization and validation
in the trusted backend before changing

32
00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:02.286
records, sending messages, storing files,

33
00:02:02.366 --> 00:02:05.460
or triggering important workflow
transitions.
